#
# prima-materia — the Thelemic transmission
#
# Order of the file: the tradition and the contact question; the works of the
# corpus as identifiers; the demand for a written record, which is what this
# transmission puts in front of everything else; the practices that carry a
# stated measure; the capacities they are said to yield; the prerequisite
# chains; the signs of success, kept apart from the failure conditions; the
# cautions, including the ones the corpus turns against its own prescriptions;
# and last the passages in which the same corpus withdraws the testability it
# demanded, which is the finding this entry exists for.
#
# The tradition instance was registered in traditions/registry-modern.ttl and
# is moved here now that passages have been read.
#
# Only the public-domain part of the corpus is opened. The instructions of the
# Equinox, volume one, numbers one to ten, London 1909 to 1913, and the second
# part of Book 4, Wieland 1913, are read at the wording. Magick in Theory and
# Practice, Paris 1929, is not: the access finding stands at its work node and
# at a search node.
#

pmt:Thelema a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Thelema"@en, "Thelema"@de ;
    skos:definition "Crowley's transmission of a received book, of the discovery of one's own will as the central task, and of a practice curriculum with a demand for recorded results."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run at the wording. The Equinox, volume one, numbers one to ten, London 1909 to 1913: Liber XXX Librae, Liber IX E vel Exercitiorum whole, Liber VI O vel Manus et Sagittae whole, Liber III vel Jugorum whole with its plate, Liber CCCXLI HHH whole, Liber XVI Turris vel Domus Dei whole, Liber CCVI RV vel Spiritus whole with its plate and the editorial note to it, Liber DXXXVI Batrachophrenoboocosmomachia whole, and A Syllabus of the Official Instructions of A∴A∴ Hitherto Published at number ten. Book 4, Part I: Meditation, chapters I, V, VI and VII. Not read at the wording: the second part of Book 4 beyond its preliminary matter, the whole of The Temple of Solomon the King, Liber CCCCXVIII, Liber XIII vel Graduum Montis Abiegni, Liber CCCCXII A vel Armorum, Liber CC Resh vel Helios, the Class A books, and the verse, prose and reviews that make up most of the ten numbers."@en ;
    skos:note "Two carriers were used for the Equinox and the reading rests on both. The first is the Celephais Press and Ordo Templi Orientis re-setting of the ten numbers, which reproduces the title pages, the running heads and the original pagination and states its own provenance in the text. The second is a page scan of number one. Where a wording matters it was collated between them; Liber E I, 7 reads intelligibly prepared in both, and the reading of Liber E III, 9 on the saucer of water is identical in both. The re-setting renders the arabic nine as g throughout, so a section number taken from it alone is not to be trusted, and every section number in this file was checked against its neighbours in the sequence."@en ;
    skos:note "Book 4, Part I was read in a text that reaches the first edition through a later one, and the nodes from it say so in the source. No scan of the Wieland print of 1912 was found; the one archive identifier offering that part returns an optical transcription in Cyrillic characters and is unusable. The part title also differs between the editions: the 1912 half-title reads Meditation, and Mysticism is the later name. A node citing Book 4 Part I Mysticism would be citing an edition that did not exist in 1912."@en ;
    skos:note "The contact route stays documented and the reading did not move it. The route is in the corpus itself and needs no secondary literature: the course of reading at Liber E VII, 2 prescribes the Upanishads, the Bhagavadgītā, the Shiva Saṃhitā, the aphorisms of Patañjali, the Haṭhayogapradīpikā, Vivekananda's Raja Yoga, the Voice of the Silence, Lévi, the Abramelin book and the Goetia by name; Liber E III names four postures and Liber E IV the alternating nostril cycles; Liber CCVI names Pingala, Ida, Suṣumṇā and the Viśuddhi cakra; the fifth number prints a treatise by Ananda Metteyya in the same issue as Liber HHH; and the second and third numbers print the neophyte ritual of the Golden Dawn, which the fourth number's editorial defends having published. A transmission whose reading list, whose postures, whose breath vocabulary and whose ritual forms all run to corpora this holding already carries cannot be counted as an independent witness for anything they share."@en ;
    skos:note "What the entry is for, and it is not the capacity list. This transmission is the one in the holding that makes the demand for a written record from inside itself, states a base rate against which a result is to be judged, publishes a negative assessment of a named practitioner, and then, in the same volumes, withdraws the testability it demanded. Both halves are entered. The withdrawal is not a later recantation and not the work of a critic: it stands three pages from the demand, in the same instruction."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Crowley, Liber AL vel Legis; Crowley, Magick in Theory and Practice; Crowley, The Vision and the Voice; the instructions of the A∴A∴ published in The Equinox, volume one, numbers one to ten; Crowley and Sturges, Book 4 (Liber ABA)" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum, pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae, pmw:LiberIIIVelJugorum, pmw:LiberHHH, pmw:LiberTurrisVelDomusDei, pmw:LiberRVVelSpiritus, pmw:LiberBatrachophrenoboocosmomachia, pmw:SyllabusInstructionumAA, pmw:LiberABA, pmw:LiberALVelLegis, pmw:LiberXXXAerum, pmw:MagickInTheoryAndPractice, pmw:LiberGraduumMontisAbiegni ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. I, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), p. 25" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum .

#
# The works of the corpus as identifiers. Which of them has been opened is not
# stated here; it follows from pm:fromWork.
#

pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX"@en, "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX"@de ;
    skos:note "Class B by the syllabus of the transmission, that is, no claim of textual invariance. Sections and roman chapter numbers are carried by the Equinox print and are absent from the reprint in the appendix of Magick in Theory and Practice, which makes the Equinox print the only carrier with a usable internal address."@en .

pmw:LiberOVelManusEtSagittae a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI"@en, "Liber O vel Manus et Sagittae sub figura VI"@de ;
    skos:note "Class B. The syllabus says of it that its instructions are too loose to find place among the official rituals, which is the transmission's own grading of the text this file leans on most."@en .

pmw:LiberIIIVelJugorum a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber III vel Jugorum"@en, "Liber III vel Jugorum"@de ;
    skos:note "Class D, an official ritual and instruction. Published with a photographic plate of a practitioner's forearm and a printed verdict on his performance."@en .

pmw:LiberHHH a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber HHH sub figura CCCXLI"@en, "Liber HHH sub figura CCCXLI"@de ;
    skos:note "Class D."@en .

pmw:LiberTurrisVelDomusDei a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber Turris vel Domus Dei sub figura XVI"@en, "Liber Turris vel Domus Dei sub figura XVI"@de ;
    skos:note "Class B."@en .

pmw:LiberRVVelSpiritus a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber RV vel Spiritus sub figura CCVI"@en, "Liber RV vel Spiritus sub figura CCVI"@de ;
    skos:note "Class B. Published with a photographic plate and an editorial note stating why the plate was necessary."@en .

pmw:LiberBatrachophrenoboocosmomachia a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber Batrachophrenoboocosmomachia sub figura DXXXVI"@en, "Liber Batrachophrenoboocosmomachia sub figura DXXXVI"@de ;
    skos:note "Class B."@en .

pmw:SyllabusInstructionumAA a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "A Syllabus of the Official Instructions of A∴A∴ Hitherto Published"@en, "A Syllabus of the Official Instructions of A∴A∴ Hitherto Published"@de ;
    skos:note "The transmission's own catalogue of its instructions, with the four classes that decide whether a text may be altered. It is the document that makes the edition of every other node in this file a necessary part of the citation."@en .

pmw:LiberABA a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Book 4 (Liber ABA)"@en, "Book 4 (Liber ABA)"@de ;
    skos:note "Published in parts: Part I, Meditation, Wieland 1912, and Part II, Magick, Wieland 1913. The title page and the opening note name two authors and state that the book is not the work of the first of them but was written down from his dictation by the second and rearranged by her, and that it was read before printing by several persons of less than average intelligence so that anything unclear to them could be elucidated. A node from this work reports a redacted dictation with a third party interposed for intelligibility, not an author's text."@en .

pmw:LiberALVelLegis a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber L vel Legis sub figura CCXX"@en, "Liber L vel Legis sub figura CCXX"@de ;
    skos:note "Class A, the only class of which the transmission claims that not so much as the style of a letter may be changed. Printed with a facsimile in the seventh number. Unopened in this holding: the received-wording question belongs to pm:Receiving and was not carried in this run."@en .

pmw:LiberXXXAerum a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber XXX Aerum vel Saeculi (The Vision and the Voice) sub figura CCCCXVIII"@en, "Liber XXX Aerum vel Saeculi (Die Vision und die Stimme) sub figura CCCCXVIII"@de ;
    skos:note "Unopened in this holding. Printed as the supplement to the fifth number and therefore reachable; the syllabus grades it A-B and says the cries of the angels are to be regarded as accurate, which makes it the place where this transmission's claim of textual reliability is strongest and least examined."@en .

pmw:MagickInTheoryAndPractice a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Magick in Theory and Practice (Book 4, Part III)"@en, "Magick in Theory and Practice (Book 4, Teil III)"@de ;
    skos:note "Unopened at the wording of its own print. The Lecram Press edition, Paris 1929, was sought and not obtained; the finding stands at a search node. What was consulted instead is an electronic edition following a later reprint, and it is used in this file for one purpose only, as the second side of a dispute about a reading."@en .

pmw:LiberGraduumMontisAbiegni a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber XIII vel Graduum Montis Abiegni"@en, "Liber XIII vel Graduum Montis Abiegni"@de ;
    skos:note "Unopened in this holding. Printed in the third number and reachable; it carries the examination order of the whole curriculum, which is the prerequisite chain this file enters only in the fragments the other instructions state for themselves."@en .

#
# The demand this transmission puts in front of everything else
#

pmp:ThelemaScriptumRecordum a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The written record of every experiment"@en, "Das schriftliche Protokoll jedes Versuchs"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "the magical record"@en ;
    skos:definition "The practice the instruction opens and closes with. Every experiment is to be recorded in detail during or immediately after its performance; the physical and mental condition of the experimenter, the time, the place, the state of the weather and all conditions that might act on the result or be sources of error are to be noted; and the more scientific the record, the better, while the emotions are to be noted as being among the conditions."@en ;
    skos:note "Two features make this more than an exhortation. It carries a sanction: the order will take no official notice of experiments not thus recorded. And it names a published model of the form, a diary printed in the same number of the journal, so that the standard is exhibited rather than described. The instruction returns to it at its end and calls the written record the only possible check upon error derived from the various qualities of the experimenter."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. I, §§ 1-4 and 7-9, and chap. VII, § 8, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), pp. 25 and 34; read in the Celephais Press and O.T.O. re-setting of the ten numbers and collated against a page scan of number one" .

pmc:ThelemaSanctioNonNotitiae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "What is not recorded is not noticed"@en, "Was nicht protokolliert ist, wird nicht zur Kenntnis genommen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The sanction attached to the record: the order states that it will take no official notice of any experiments which are not properly recorded in the manner the three preceding sections set out."@en ;
    skos:note "The rule is entered separately from the practice because it is the part that can be complied with or broken and because it is the transmission's own answer to the question this holding asks of every claim. It makes the record the condition of admissibility rather than a matter of diligence, which is the same move a protocol makes here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. I, § 4, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), p. 25" .

pmc:ThelemaClassesInstructionum a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "The four classes of publication, and which of them may be altered"@en, "Die vier Publikationsklassen, und welche von ihnen geändert werden darf"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission's own ordering of its instructions into four classes: class A consists of books of which not so much as the style of a letter may be changed, being beyond the criticism even of the visible head of the organisation; class B of books or essays that are the result of ordinary scholarship; class C of matter to be regarded as suggestive; class D of the official rituals and instructions. Some publications are composite and belong to more than one class."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the ordering act that governs how every other node in this file must cite. Of the instructions read here, none is class A: Liber E, Liber O, Liber Turris, Liber RV and Liber DXXXVI are class B, Liber HHH and Liber III class D. For all of them the transmission itself asserts no textual invariance, and the edition read is therefore part of the claim and not a courtesy. The one book that does claim invariance is the received one, which this run did not open."@en ;
    skos:note "It is entered as an ordering act and not as a doctrine because the classes are a classification imposed on texts already published, and because the same document that states them lists which books fall where, with a reason in each case."@en ;
    pm:ordersClaim pmc:ThelemaSanctioNonNotitiae ;
    pm:compiledBy "The A∴A∴, in the syllabus signed by its three officers" ;
    dcterms:date "1913" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:SyllabusInstructionumAA ;
    dcterms:source "A Syllabus of the Official Instructions of A∴A∴ Hitherto Published, in The Equinox I,10 (London, September 1913), p. 43" .

#
# The practices that carry a stated measure
#

pmp:ThelemaProbatioTarot a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The card test with a tabulated result"@en, "Die Kartenprobe mit tabelliertem Ergebnis"@de ;
    skos:definition "Take a pack of seventy-eight tarot cards, shuffle, cut, draw one card, and without looking at it try to name it. Write down the card named and the actual card; repeat and tabulate the results. The easier form, guessing only the suit, is to be tried first, and all possibilities of obtaining the knowledge through sight, touch or smell are to be excluded."@en ;
    skos:note "The exclusion clause is not a formality and the instruction says why: there was once a man whose fingertips were so sensitive that he could feel the shape and position of the pips and so judge the card correctly. A stated source of error with a stated mechanism is rare in this holding."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. II, §§ 1-4, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), pp. 26-27" .

pmc:ThelemaRatioCasus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The stated chance rate, and the threshold above it"@en, "Die genannte Zufallsrate und die Schwelle darüber"@de ;
    skos:definition "The instruction states the rate to be expected without any clairvoyance at all: the right card once in seventy-eight times; and, since seventy-eight cards yield twenty-two trumps and fourteen of each other suit, a correct suit roughly twice in seven times by calling trumps every time. Against that it sets the threshold at which the practitioner may be admitted for examination: the suit correctly three times in four, very few inharmonious errors, and the card named aright as many as fifteen or twenty times in seventy-eight."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the only passage the run found anywhere in the corpus that puts a number on chance and a second number on success. Both are stated by the transmission and neither is the compiler's. It is what the testability-driven entrance of the specification is looking for, and it is here rather than in a tradition with a longer pedigree."@en ;
    skos:note "The second of the two chance figures is also a stated strategy for beating a naive score without any faculty at all, which means the transmission had thought about the base rate rather than merely quoting one."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. II, §§ 3, 5 and 8-9, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), pp. 26-27" .

pmc:ThelemaClarividentiaPhysica a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Physical clairvoyance, as this transmission defines it"@en, "Physische Hellsicht, wie diese Überlieferung sie bestimmt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the card test is held to measure: naming a drawn card whose face has not been seen, at a rate above the chance rate the same instruction states. The instruction's own heading for the chapter is physical clairvoyance."@en ;
    skos:note "Defined by its measurement and by nothing else, which is why it is the one capacity in this file that a count could settle. No mechanism is offered and none is needed for the claim as stated."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. II, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), pp. 26-27" .

pmc:ThelemaProbatioTarotYieldsClarividentia a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The card practice is said to develop the faculty it measures"@en, "Die Kartenpraxis soll das Vermögen ausbilden, das sie misst"@de ;
    skos:definition "The instruction is written as a progression and not only as a test: as you progress, you will find that you are able to distinguish the suit correctly three times in four, and in seventy-eight experiments to name the card aright as many as fifteen or twenty times. The practice is thus held to bring about the capacity, and at the threshold the practitioner is admitted for examination and given more complex exercises."@en ;
    skos:note "The double role is worth holding as such. A procedure that is at once the training and the measurement cannot separate an improvement in the faculty from an improvement in the procedure, and the instruction does not distinguish them. That is a property of the claim and not a defect of the reading."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ThelemaProbatioTarot ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ThelemaClarividentiaPhysica ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. II, §§ 8-9, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), p. 27" .

pmp:ThelemaQuattuorSedes a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The four postures, held with every muscle tense"@en, "Die vier Stellungen, mit jedem Muskel angespannt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Learning to sit perfectly still with every muscle tense for long periods, in one of four named positions: the God, seated in a chair with head up, back straight, knees together, hands on knees and eyes closed; the Dragon, kneeling with the buttocks on the heels and the toes turned back; the Ibis, standing and holding the left ankle with the right hand, the free forefinger on the lips; the Thunderbolt, seated with the left heel pressing up the anus, the right foot poised on its toes and the arms stretched over the knees. No garment is worn that interferes with the posture."@en ;
    skos:note "The duration of the practice, the severity of the pain if any, and the degree of rigidity attained are to be noted down, so the posture is one of the places where the record has something countable to hold."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. III, §§ 1-8, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), pp. 27-28" .

pmc:ThelemaMensuraPatinae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The saucer of water as the measure of the posture"@en, "Die Untertasse Wasser als Maß der Stellung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The stated criterion for the posture: a saucer filled to the brim with water and poised upon the head does not spill one drop during a whole hour, and no tremor can any longer be perceived in any muscle. At that point the practitioner is admitted for examination."@en ;
    skos:note "A criterion a bystander can apply, unlike almost everything else this transmission offers as a sign, and one that fails visibly. It is entered as its own node for that reason: the posture and the measure of the posture are separable, and only the second is examinable."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. III, § 9, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), p. 28; the wording collated between the re-setting and a page scan of the number" .

pmp:ThelemaPranayamaNumerata a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The counted breath cycles"@en, "Die gezählten Atemzyklen"@de ;
    skos:definition "At rest in one of the postures, the right nostril closed with the thumb, breathing out slowly and completely through the left nostril while the watch marks twenty seconds, and in through the same nostril for ten, then changing hands and repeating with the other nostril, continuous for one hour. Then, when each stage is quite easy and not before, the periods rise: thirty and fifteen; out fifteen, in fifteen, held fifteen; out forty, in twenty; out twenty, in ten, held thirty."@en ;
    skos:note "Two conditions are stated with it. Food in the stomach, even in small quantities, makes the practice very difficult; and the practitioner is never to overstrain, in particular never to get so short of breath that he is compelled to breathe out jerkily or rapidly. The second is both a caution and a failure indicator, and it is entered again as a caution below."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. IV, §§ 1-9, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), p. 29" .

pmp:ThelemaDharanaNumerata a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Concentration with the interruptions counted"@en, "Konzentration mit gezählten Unterbrechungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Constraining the mind to a single simple imagined object, then to combinations, then to simple moving objects and to combinations of moving objects, with no other thought allowed to intrude. Seated in the posture, with notebook and pencil at one's side and a watch in front of one, at first for not more than ten minutes at a time; the duration, the number and nature of the intruding thoughts and the tendency of the object to depart from the course laid out for it are noted. The number of times the thought wanders is counted, on the fingers or on a string of beads."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the practice that turns the record into an instrument. The counted unit is the break, and the count is kept while the practice runs rather than reconstructed afterwards. The later exposition states the profile expected at a given stage: an average practice of about half an hour with between ten and twenty breaks."@en ;
    skos:note "It also supplies the one case in the holding of a break being externally observable. A footnote states that the grosser kind of break can be detected by another person, that it is accompanied by a flickering of the eyelid and can be seen by him, and that with practice he could detect even very small breaks."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Thelema ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberEVelExercitiorum ;
    dcterms:source "Liber E vel Exercitiorum sub figura IX, chap. V, §§ 1-6, in The Equinox I,1 (London, March 1909), p. 30, with the counting procedure and the expected profile at Book 4 (Liber ABA), Part I: Meditation, chap. V, pp. 58-61 (first published London: Wieland & Co., 1912; read in the Celephais Press electronic edition of 2004, which follows the corrected text in Magick: Book 4 Parts I-IV, York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1994)" .